    "question": "A relation R on a set is known to be symmetric and transitive. Which property is NOT guaranteed for R?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Reflexive",
      "B": "Symmetric",
      "C": "Transitive",
      "D": "None of the above"
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "Symmetry and transitivity do not imply reflexivity. A relation can be symmetric and transitive without containing every (a, a) pair, so reflexivity is not guaranteed.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "If set A has m elements and set B has n elements, how many distinct functions f : A → B exist?",
    "options": {
      "A": "nᵐ",
      "B": "mⁿ",
      "C": "m! · n!",
      "D": "(m + n)!"
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "For each of the m elements of A, the function can choose any of the n  elements of B as its image. Hence the total number of functions is nᵐ.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Which of the following statements is always true for a bijective function f : A → B?",
    "options": {
      "A": "An inverse function f⁻¹ exists",
      "B": "The domain and codomain have the same number of elements",
      "C": "The function is both injective and surjective",
      "D": "All of the above"
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "A bijective function is, by definition, both injective and surjective, which implies the domain and codomain must have equal cardinality, and guarantees the existence of a two‑sided inverse.",
